          %h1#nondeterministic_automata Non-deterministic automata
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definitions"} Definitions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#coalgebraic_description"} Coalgebraic description
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            (For the moment, mostly as an example of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra+for+an+endofunctor"} coalgebra for an endofunctor
            and to start discussion of models for non-determinism.)
          %p This will give the classical definition as a non-deterministic state-based system and then show how to turn that form into the coalgebraic form.
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                non-deterministic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automaton"} automaton
              consists of the following data:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em states
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Q
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              %li
                %p
                  a set,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Σ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                  %em input symbols
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  a function,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi δ
                          %mo :
                          %mi Q
                          %mo ×
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo →
                          %mi 𝒫
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Q
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta : Q\times \Sigma \to \mathcal{P}(Q)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em next state relation
            %p and
            %ul
              %li
                a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi final
                        %mo :
                        %mi Q
                        %mo →
                        %mi bool
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ⊤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} final : Q \to bool = \{\bot, \top\}
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+domain"} boolean domain
          %p
            In the usual interpretation if the automaton is in state
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi q
                    %mo ,
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(q,\sigma)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
          %p
            The ‘final’ predicate, of course, returns
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            if a state is a
            %em final state
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            otherwise. (There will, in general, be a set of final or ‘accept’ states.)
          %p (For the moment we will not look at the links between automata and languages.)
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#coalgebraic_description Coalgebraic description
          %p
            The first step in transforming the above definition to the coalgebraic form is to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/currying"} curry
            = succeed "," do
